{"type":"tool_call","subtype":"started","call_id":"tool_96148904-896b-4ab2-97e1-8c1623e4d26","tool_call":{"editToolCall":{"args":{"path":"/tmp/world-experiment/npdes-conversational-semantic-review-v1/a9d807a403677a42c31ac0b8d1a66aeb/REPLY.md","streamContent":"No — “exactly one applicable numeric limit per measurement” is not a safe general rule. A single reported value can legitimately need to be checked against more than one limit at once, most often because the permit sets different caps for different kinds of limits on the same pollutant (for example a daily maximum and a monthly average, or a mass limit alongside a concentration limit). When date ranges overlap or one limit replaces another mid-year, the question is usually not “which row wins?” but “which limit type does this measurement actually represent?” Staged or overlapping intervals often mean the permit changed during the year, or different limit types are in force at the same time — not that you should arbitrarily pick one row.\n\nWhen the catalog shows what looks like the same limit twice, I would not assume two separate obligations by default. Many duplicates are restatements, schedule variants, or leftover rows from permit amendments. Treat them as one limit unless the numbers, units, or the kind of limit (instantaneous vs average, mass vs concentration, etc.) genuinely differ. Deduplicate on what the limit actually requires, not on how many times it appears in the extract.\n\nThe bigger gap, though, is matching the measurement to the right *kind* of limit before you count how many apply. A grab sample, a daily average, and a monthly average are not interchangeable even for the same outfall and parameter — and a limit that was superseded should drop out even if its dates still look plausible. So instead of enforcing one pair per measurement, I’d keep all limits that are in force for that period and limit type, drop obvious catalog duplicates, and only then decide whether you have zero, one, or several real comparisons to make.\n"}},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"tool_96148904-896b-4ab2-97e1-8c1623e4d26","startedAtMs":"1788592230155"},"model_call_id":"ee589e41-f674-448e-8d64-158ed7b5731c-1-04i9","session_id":"afe82330-bf6d-4d55-a867-cfda8e6ef4cd","timestamp_ms":1788592230215}
